</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Word-level Sense Composition</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous sections, we introduced token-level sense embeddings (Section 3.1) and word-level lexical relations (Section 3.2). In practice, however, a word may correspond to either a single token or multiple tokens depending on the tokenizer. When a word is represented by a single token, its sense embeddings are directly given by the token-level sense dictionary. For words that are decomposed into multiple tokens by the tokenizer, we construct word-level sense embeddings by composing the sense embeddings of their constituent tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let a word <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> be tokenized into a sequence of tokens <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},t_{2},\ldots,t_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of tokens associated with the word. Recall that each token can have <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> senses. If <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the word-level sense embeddings are given directly by the sense embeddings of the only token <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Otherwise, we compose sense embeddings iteratively across the token sequence. We begin with the sense embeddings of the first token, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>. At each subsequent step <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=2,\ldots,m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=2,\ldots,m</annotation></semantics></math>, we first compute the mean of the composed sense embeddings obtained from tokens <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(t_{1},\ldots,t_{h-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(t_{1},\ldots,t_{h-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Using this mean representation, we perform nearest-neighbor matching under L2 distance with the sense embeddings <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> of the next token, which can have up to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> senses. The matched sense embeddings are then incorporated into the composed representation. After processing all tokens, the final word-level sense embeddings are obtained by averaging the aligned sense embeddings across the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> tokens:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{S}_{w}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{h=1}^{m}\text{aligned}(S_{t_{h}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><mrow><mtext>aligned</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{S}_{w}=\frac{1}{m}\sum_{h=1}^{m}\text{aligned}(S_{t_{h}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">This formulation ensures that each token contributes equally to the final word-level representation, avoids exponential attenuation of early tokens, and allows later tokens to iteratively refine sense alignment through nearest-neighbor matching. The resulting word-level sense embeddings provide a coherent semantic representation for words composed of multiple tokens. While this procedure describes the construction of a single
composite sense, we apply this composition independently for each of
the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> token-level senses associated with the word. As a result, each word is represented up to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> sense embeddings. Furthermore, we only decompose the word occurs in synonym and antonym pairs and incorporate them into our sense dictionary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To bound the complexity of word-level composition during dictionary construction, we limit the maximum number of tokens per word to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3. Word-level Sense Composition ‣ 3. DSKD Sense Dictionary ‣ Decoder-based Sense Knowledge Distillation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports the keep rates of synonym and antonym relations under different values of <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, where the keep rate denotes the percentage of tokenized lexical relations whose word token length does not exceed <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in the table, setting <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math> already retains over 90% of synonym antonym relations for both tokenizers. Increasing <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> beyond this point yields only marginal improvements in coverage while substantially increasing computational cost. Based on this analysis, we set <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math> in all experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
